4.1  Process states and zombies

TL;DR
  - Every process has a one-letter state: R running, S sleeping, D waiting on
    I/O, T stopped, Z zombie.
  - Most processes are S, waiting for something. That's normal.
  - A zombie has already exited. Only its entry is left, until its parent reads
    how it ended.
  - Zombies use no CPU and almost no memory, but each one holds a PID.
  - You can't kill a zombie: fix or restart its parent.

      ps -eo pid,ppid,stat,comm | awk '$3 ~ /^Z/'

On your machine
  Total    8 processes: 1 running, 3 sleeping, 1 waiting on I/O (D), 1 idle
           kernel thread, 2 zombies
  Zombies  PID 4242 worker (old), parent 4100 supervisor
           PID 4300 orphan-job, parent 9999
  Waiting  PID 5000 rsync, parent 1 systemd

Pitfalls
  - kill -9 on a zombie does nothing: it's already dead. The PPID column tells
    you which parent to look at.
  - A few zombies that come and go are normal. A count that keeps growing means
    a parent that never collects its children.
  - A process in D usually ignores even kill -9 until its I/O finishes. Many of
    them at once often points to a slow disk or a hung network mount.
  - D processes count in the load average, so the load can be high while the
    CPUs sit idle.
  - ps adds letters after the state: s session leader, + foreground, < high
    priority, N low priority, l multithreaded.

Deep dive: 3 questions. Read them with idk 4.1 --why

Related
  Commands  ps, top
  Paths     /proc/PID/stat, /proc/PID/status
